1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,280
 Hi, in this lesson, I will talk about approaches to build an app with an AI editor.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:08,500
 Let's get started.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:12,200
 And before you begin building a complete application with AI,

4
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,720
 it's important to decide how you want to work.

5
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:19,200
 A good approach helps you move faster, avoid mistakes,

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,080
 and stay in control of your final result.

7
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:28,200
 Different projects and situations call for different workflows.

8
00:00:28,200 --> 00:00:31,600
 In this lesson, you'll learn four main approaches,

9
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,480
 and when each of them is the best choice.

10
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:38,000
 Let's start with the step-by-step approach.

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,920
 In this method, you work with the AI by giving one shot task at a time.

12
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,400
 You review every update before moving to the next one,

13
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:51,320
 so you keep maximum control and minimize risk.

14
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:54,480
 For example, you could ask the AI to create a VIT,

15
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:58,960
 React TypeScript app named currency converter.

16
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:04,200
 Then add a currency converter component with an amount input to currency selects,

17
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:08,120
 a swap button, and a convert button.

18
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:12,120
 Next, request simple CSS for a clean design.

19
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:18,160
 A white card with rounded corners, a soft shadow, and centered content.

20
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:20,880
 After that, add marked exchange rate data

21
00:01:20,880 --> 00:01:25,480
 and show the conversion result when the button is clicked.

22
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:32,120
 Finally, implement the swap logic for currencies and input validation for empty values.

23
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:34,840
 The advantages of this approach are full control,

24
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:38,960
 complete transparency, and very low risk of breaking things.

25
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:43,040
 It's also easy to review and fix small errors immediately.

26
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,840
 The downsides of slower progress, many prompts,

27
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:50,120
 and the chance of losing direction if you don't have a plan.

28
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:53,840
 Use this approach for careful updates in real projects,

29
00:01:53,840 --> 00:01:59,680
 hot fixes, small improvements, or refactoring one screen or feature at a time.

30
00:01:59,680 --> 00:02:03,600
 Now let's move to the design first or UI-driven approach.

31
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:10,680
 Here, you upload a screenshot or describe the layout and the AI builds the interface first.

32
00:02:10,680 --> 00:02:17,160
 This is great for quick visual results or when design matters more than functionality early on.

33
00:02:17,160 --> 00:02:21,400
 For example, you might ask the AI to build your apps user interface

34
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:27,080
 from a screenshot using vit plus react plus TypeScript.

35
00:02:27,080 --> 00:02:30,120
 You can tell it to keep the CSS simple,

36
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:35,680
 include flags in the select, and make a clean, centered card layout.

37
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:41,640
 The pros of this approach are fast visual progress and easy matching to a specific design.

38
00:02:41,640 --> 00:02:43,920
 The cons are that logic is added later,

39
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:47,760
 which might lead to rework and without clear direction,

40
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:51,320
 the AI might structure code problem.

41
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:55,920
 And this approach is best for converting Figma or screenshots into code,

42
00:02:55,920 --> 00:03:00,680
 rapid demos, prototypes, landing pages, or marketing pages.

43
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:02,920
 Next is the plan first approach.

44
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:09,640
 With this method, you start by creating a detailed structured plan with the AI before writing any code.

45
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:13,880
 Once you approve the plan, the AI builds the project according to that structure.

46
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:22,960
 For example, you can ask the AI to create a step-by-step plan to build the currency converter up using vit plus react plus TypeScript.

47
00:03:22,960 --> 00:03:29,040
 The app should convert and enter the mound from one currency to another using mock rates,

48
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:34,440
 allow swapping currencies, and display a result in expected format.

49
00:03:34,440 --> 00:03:41,560
 The advantages are a clear structure from the beginning, fewer mistakes, and better quality results.

50
00:03:41,560 --> 00:03:46,920
 It's especially good for projects with multiple components or files.

51
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:54,200
 The disadvantages are a longer preparation time and the need for detailed instructions to get an accurate plan.

52
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:57,320
 This approach works best when starting a new app,

53
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:02,400
 building large features, or working on UI, logic, and tests together.

54
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,240
 Finally, let's talk about the mixed approach.

55
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:12,600
 This combines planning, design, and detailed steps for a balanced workflow.

56
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:17,600
 You start by providing both a screenshot and your functional requirements,

57
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:21,400
 then ask the AI to create a plan that marches them.

58
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:27,360
 This gives you the speed of the design first approach and the safety of the plan first method.

59
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:36,160
 For example, you might ask the AI to create a step-by-step plan to build your app from a screenshot using vit plus react plus TypeScript.

60
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:43,160
 And the app should convert and enter the mount from one currency to another using mock rates,

61
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:48,160
 allow swapping currencies, and show a result in specific format.

62
00:04:48,160 --> 00:04:55,160
 Also, keep the CSS simple, include flags in the selects and use a clean centered card layout.

63
00:04:55,160 --> 00:05:00,160
 The mixed approach offers a great balance between speed and structure.

64
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:06,160
 With less rework, then UI only methods and strong control with visual clarity.

65
00:05:06,160 --> 00:05:13,160
 Its downsides are that it takes more steps than design first and still requires reviews at each stage.

66
00:05:13,160 --> 00:05:17,160
 Its ideal when your UI is ready, but logic is also important.

67
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:25,160
 For features that touch multiple parts of the app or when you want reliable results quickly, final comparison.

68
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:32,160
 Step-by-step gives maximum safety, but moves slowly. If you need total control, choose step-by-step.

69
00:05:32,160 --> 00:05:38,160
 Design first is fast and visually impressive, but may require later fixes.

70
00:05:38,160 --> 00:05:42,160
 If you want fast visible progress, go with design first.

71
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:48,160
 Plan first builds a clean, organized project, but takes more setup time.

72
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:52,160
 If you prefer well-structured, maintainable code, use plan first.

73
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:57,160
 Mixed combines fast visuals with solid structure for the best balance.

74
00:05:57,160 --> 00:06:01,160
 If you want both speed and reliability, mixed is your best choice.

75
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:03,160
 Let's wrap up.

76
00:06:03,160 --> 00:06:09,160
 Choosing the right approach early makes development smoother, faster, and cleaner.

77
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:16,160
 Pick the method that fits your design status, features scope, and how much control you want.

78
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:23,160
 You can even combine approaches in a single project to balance speed and quality.

79
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:28,160
 Good workflow choice means faster delivery and better code quality.

80
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:33,160
 I hope this lesson was useful for you and see you in the next lesson.

